<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mandate of Heaven</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tianming</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mandate of Heaven, tianming in Chinese, is the doctrine that Heaven grants the right to rule to a virtuous ruler and withdraws it from a wicked or incompetent one. It was formulated by the early Zhou to justify their overthrow of the Shang dynasty around 1046 BCE. Later Chinese thinkers and dynasties used it to explain the rise and fall of ruling houses, treating rebellion, disasters and defeat as signs that the mandate had been lost.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mandate-of-heaven.jpg" alt="Mandate of Heaven" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peenwald · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dynastic_Cycle.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Zhou conquerors absorbed Shang scribes, craftsmen and ritual specialists and justified their own rule by contrasting the virtue of their founders with the alleged depravity of the last Shang king, the origin of the theory of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shang-dynasty">Shang dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The doctrine of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> (tianming), set out in chapters of the Book of Documents attributed to the Duke of Zhou, held that Heaven entrusted rule to the virtuous and withdrew it from the wicked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-zhou">Western Zhou · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…physical heavens and the home of gods and ancestors, and the source of moral meaning, as in the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>; qi, the breath or energy of which all things are made, gods included; the veneration of ancestors; and bao ying, moral reciprocity, by which every being is…</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Shulgi of Ur declared himself divine in the 21st century BCE, the pharaohs of Egypt were regarded as gods on earth, Chinese emperors ruled by the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b>, and the Sapa Inca was revered as the son of the sun god Inti.</blockquote><a href="/government/absolute-monarchy">Absolute monarchy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Purpose was the hardest to judge, since almost every ruler claimed to serve the common good; in China the doctrine of the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> left the judgement to Heaven, which withdrew the right to rule from a wicked or incompetent ruler.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-should-rule">Who Should Rule?</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Mandate of Heaven</b> the right to rule rested on virtue, not birth: the Ming founder, the Hongwu Emperor, had been a penniless peasant and Buddhist monk.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
